<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Murong Jun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">319–360</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Emperor Jingzhao of Former Yan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-xianbei">Murong</a> Jun was the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/former-yan">Former Yan</a> from 348 to 360 and son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-huang">Murong Huang</a>. After the collapse of Later Zhao he led his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> armies onto the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china-plain">North China Plain</a>, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ran-min">Ran Min</a> in 352 and proclaimed himself emperor. He moved his capital to Ye in 357, making Former Yan one of the great powers of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> alongside Former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Murong Jun, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> name was Helaiba, was born in 319, while his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-huang">Murong Huang</a> was still heir to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-hui">Murong Hui</a>, the Jin-created Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a>. He was made heir apparent after his father succeeded in 333, and kept that rank when Murong Huang claimed the title Prince of Yan in 337. Accounts say his father once thought of naming his younger brother Murong Ba instead, which left Murong Jun jealous of him. He led an army against Dai in 344 with his uncle Murong Ping, and in 346 nominally commanded the army, led in fact by his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-ke">Murong Ke</a>, that captured the capital and king of Buyeo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After the Later Zhao emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-hu">Shi Hu</a> died in 349 and his state fell into civil war, Murong Jun attacked in the spring of 350, took Jicheng in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, moved his capital there from Longcheng and soon held all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/youzhou">Youzhou</a>. In the summer of 352 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-ke">Murong Ke</a> drew the infantry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ran-min">Ran Min</a>, founder of Ran Wei, onto the plains and broke it with cavalry; Ran was whipped, exiled and beheaded, though later buried with honours. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/former-yan">Former Yan</a> forces then breached Ye and captured Ran Zhi and Empress Dong, ending Ran Wei, and Murong Jun broke openly with the Jin.</p><p>As emperor he made his wife Kezuhun empress in 353. In 356 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-ke">Murong Ke</a> took Guanggu in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> from his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duan-kan">Duan Kan</a>, whom he executed in 357. In 358 he ordered large-scale conscription for war on Former Qin and Jin, scaled back after a petition by Liu Gui, and his rivalry with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-chui">Murong Chui</a>, as Murong Ba was now named, flared when Chui's wife Princess Duan was falsely accused of witchcraft and died in prison. A Jin attack in 359 under Zhuge You and Xie Wan was defeated, opening the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a> region to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/former-yan">Former Yan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Falling ill early in 360, Murong Jun offered the throne to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-ke">Murong Ke</a> rather than to his 10-year-old son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-wei">Murong Wei</a>, but Murong Ke declined. He entrusted the crown prince to Murong Ke, Yang Wu, Murong Ping and Muyu Gen and died on 23 February 360. During his reign the state grew from modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaoning">Liaoning</a> and part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a> to nearly all the land north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>, with substantial holdings south of it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Murong Jun” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Later Zhao collapsed, <b>Murong Jun</b> conquered the north-east plain, captured Ran Min in 352, proclaimed himself emperor and moved the capital to Ye in 357.</blockquote><a href="/state/former-yan">Former Yan</a></li></ul></section>
